fact WorkItem(id: "WI-788", description: some(value: "A destructuring binder over an OUT-OF-ORDER tuple literal binds by SOURCE POSITION while the typer typed those binders by the type's DECLARED FIELD ORDER -- the two disagree silently, and an operation declared `-> Int64` returns a String on a clean load. MEASURED end-to-end (driven directly, and independently reproduced by two review agents building pre/post binaries): `operation ap(f: Function[A = (a: Int64, b: String), B = Int64]) -> Int64 = f((b: \"ess\", a: 3))` with `operation drive() -> Int64 = ap(lambda (p, q) -> p)` LOADS CLEAN and returns `Ok(Str(\"ess\"))`. The all-Int64 variant `f((b: 10, a: 3))` with `lambda (p, q) -> p - q` returns 7 where the declared order (a, b) implies -7; a three-field scramble returns 123 where the declared order implies 321; an all-Int64 two-field case returns 12 vs 21 with no type signal at all. Reachable through a lambda binder, a `match` case, and `let`-destructuring alike. ROOT CAUSE: `align_named_tuple_fields` (kb/typing.rs) relates a tuple LITERAL's type to a tuple TYPE **by name, order-insensitively** -- the by-name rung is tried first -- so a permuted literal type-checks against the declared type and the typer assigns binder *i* the type of DECLARED field *i*. Meanwhile `match_tuple_pattern` (eval/pattern.rs, WI-785) binds binder *i* to the value's SOURCE component *i*. Where written order differs from declared order, the type the typer gave a binder is not the type of the value it receives. NOT FIXABLE IN EVAL: the matcher never sees the type, and the value carrier is a faithful ordered product (WI-786 guarantees `pos ++ named` is source order) -- the defect is entirely in the TYPE RELATION admitting a permutation that the value representation does not perform. THIS IS A REGRESSION OF WI-785: before it, a name-keyed tuple presented as ZERO positional components, so the old `pos.len() != positional.len()` test refused the match and raised LOUDLY. WI-785 correctly taught the matcher to read name-keyed tuples and thereby converted this shape from a loud failure into a quiet wrong answer. Reverting WI-785 is not the answer -- it fixed a real bug and its own tests pin it -- the type relation must stop admitting the permutation for this consumer. SAME DEFECT AS WI-783 AT A DIFFERENT SITE: WI-783 (Delivered) has a live, confirmed permuted-ARROW regression -- `impl_ba(b, a)` passed where `(a, b)` is declared, with `f(b: 10, a: 3)` returning 7 while the positional spelling of the same written order returns -7 -- and its recorded root cause is the same one: `align_named_tuple_fields` aligns by name (there via `TupleAlign::ParamList`, whose by-name rung is also tried first) while the runtime binds by position. So there are now TWO live silent-wrong-answer bugs on main sharing one root cause in one function, one on the arrow-parameter-list side and one on the data-tuple side. That reframes WI-782 from a tidiness ticket into the gate on both. FIX DIRECTION: a tuple type used to type a DESTRUCTURING BINDER LIST (or an arrow parameter list) must align POSITIONALLY, or the by-name permutation must be REJECTED when the consumer destructures -- because the consumer reads by position and the carrier is an ordered product. By-name alignment remains correct for consumers that read by NAME (`t.x` field access, which WI-638 defines as order-independent); the bug is applying one relation to both kinds of consumer. Whatever WI-782 settles for parameter lists should settle this identically -- if the two are fixed separately they will drift, since they are the same rung of the same function. RELATED: WI-785 (the matcher that made this observable), WI-786 (the carrier's source-order invariant, which holds and is not at fault), WI-775 (established that a tuple's KEYING is part of its type). Found by /code-review on 93b23188. ACCEPTANCE: `f((b: \"ess\", a: 3))` against declared `(a: Int64, b: String)` either binds `p` to the Int64 component or is REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic -- it must not load clean and return a String from an `-> Int64` operation; the all-Int64 permuted cases agree with the declared order or are rejected; the fix is expressed in the SAME place as WI-782's parameter-list fix, not as a second independent rule; a regression test drives an out-of-order literal end-to-end and asserts the answer matches the DECLARED field order (or that the program is refused); WI-783's permuted-arrow repro is re-driven and agrees; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-782"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T13:39:48Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "claude", content: "WI-782 is DELIVERED, so this is unblocked; recording the measurement it produced so the ByName decision need not be re-derived. WI-782 fixed the ParamList rung ONLY: equal arity + positional correspondence (names may differ -- the _1.._n eta rung -- but may not be reordered or dropped), plus an arity-1 TUPLE-typed parameter list now keeps a one-field wrapper so a parameter list is distinguishable from a lone tuple parameter (arrow_param_slot_for_list in kb/typing.rs; surface producer in kb/load.rs; decoder in arrow_parts). The ByName rung is EXACTLY as it was, which is why this ticket is still live. INSTRUMENTED BLAST RADIUS of tightening ByName: I logged every align_named_tuple_fields call whose by-name rung succeeded but an equal-arity/same-order-names rung would have refused, across the WHOLE workspace suite. 7 firings, all ByName, in 3 places. (1) PERMUTED x2, both from wi488_producer_component_escape_test::named_tuple_reordered_component_abstracting_producer_flagged -- a DELIBERATE test, fixture 'operation mkBare(m: MemStore) -> (b: Bool, a: KVStore) = (a: m, b: true)', a permuted RETURN asserted to pass conformance and then be flagged by the WI-488 abstracting-producer gate. That fixture is itself an instance of THIS bug (carrier is source-order [a,b], declared type says [b,a]); refusing the permutation changes its premise, so it would need a non-permuted fixture plus a separate guard for its 'align by name, not a raw positional zip' intent. (2) WIDTH x5: typing_test::subtype_named_tuple_width (explicit unit test asserting (a: Int64, b: String) <: (a: Int64)), plus two load-ERROR paths that end in rejection anyway (wi766_one_component_tuple_type_test::load_errs, wi775_positional_tuple_bridge_test::assert_arg_mismatch). ZERO firings elsewhere. So this is a genuine LANGUAGE decision -- does a named tuple's field ORDER belong to its identity, and does width subtyping survive -- gated on one deliberate permutation test and one deliberate width test, NOT on broad breakage. The WI-782 feedback points at the narrower answer: make the rule depend on the CONSUMER's read discipline (positionally-consumed destructuring binder list -> positional; name-consuming t.x -> by name), which leaves both those tests intact. CORRECTION to this ticket's premise: the ARROW half did NOT reproduce. Driven directly, 'f(b: 10, a: 3)' returns -7 and 'f(10, 3)' returns 7 -- inverted from the numbers recorded here, and both CORRECT: they are different calls (the first says a=3, the second a=10), and impl_ba's own binder names never reach its eta arrow, which is keyed _1.._n. So there is ONE live silent-wrong-answer bug in this cluster (the data-tuple one, this ticket), not two.", at: "2026-07-20T09:47:02Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION to my earlier feedback on this ticket (2026-07-20): it described main as carrying an arity-1 tuple-param WRAPPER, naming arrow_param_slot_for_list / a kb/load.rs surface producer / a decoder in arrow_parts. That wrapper was REVERTED before WI-782 shipped and NONE of those symbols exist on main -- I wrote that note mid-session and did not amend it when the revert landed. Ignore that sentence. What is true on main: WI-782 shipped the ParamList rung only (equal arity + positional zip, admitted when names line up or one side is the synthetic _1.._n), and the param slot still COLLAPSES an arity-1 parameter list to its parameter's bare type, so a one-tuple-parameter op is still the same term as an n-parameter one -- that is WI-791, open. The ByName blast-radius measurement in that earlier note stands and was taken against a tree whose ByName arm is byte-identical to main: 7 firings, all ByName, in 3 places (2 PERMUTED from wi488_producer_component_escape_test::named_tuple_reordered_component_abstracting_producer_flagged; 5 WIDTH from typing_test::subtype_named_tuple_width plus two load-error paths). The correction to this ticket's arrow-side premise also stands: driven, f(b: 10, a: 3) = -7 and f(10, 3) = 7 are both CORRECT, so WI-783's recorded permuted-arrow regression does not reproduce and this cluster has ONE live silent-wrong-answer bug (this one), not two. NEW, relevant to whoever fixes this: a /code-review of the shipped WI-782 measured that the ParamList tightening FALSELY REJECTS correct data-tuple programs, because the arity-1 collapse routes a lone tuple-typed parameter into ParamList -- 'operation get_x(t: (x: Int64, y: Bool)) -> Int64 = t.x' passed to 'take(f: (u: (y: Bool, x: Int64)) -> Int64)' loaded and evaluated to 7 on the parent commit and is refused now. That is the SAME confusion as this ticket from the other side: a relation is being chosen by TYPE POSITION when it should be chosen by the CONSUMER'S READ DISCIPLINE. If this ticket is fixed by making the discipline the axis (positionally-consumed binder list -> positional; name-consuming t.x reader -> by name), it should also retire that false rejection, and TupleAlign should probably be renamed ByName/ByPosition so the enum names the discipline rather than the site.", at: "2026-07-20T10:50:43Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "user", content: "ROOT CAUSE AS RECORDED WAS INCOMPLETE, and the recorded FIX DIRECTION was refuted. Measured: the headline repro never reaches align_named_tuple_fields at all. arrow_positional_param_slots returns None for Function[A,B,E] BY DESIGN (it states no arity; WI-775 settled that f(3,10) and f((3,10)) are both legal there), and 'no count can be REQUIRED' had been taken to mean 'nothing can be checked' -- so a function-value application at a Function slot checked NOTHING. Proof it is not a permutation gap: the IN-ORDER but type-wrong f((a: \"ess\", b: 3)) also loaded clean and returned Str from -> Int64, and f(true) loaded clean too. So there were TWO independent defects; the ticket recorded one. FIX DIRECTION REFUTED: 'align by the CONSUMER's read discipline' is not decidable where the permutation is admitted -- the value flows through a Function[A,B] PARAMETER to a consumer chosen at a DIFFERENT call site (ap(lambda (p,q) -> p) vs ap(lambda t -> t.a)), so the site relating the literal to A cannot know which reader it will meet. DECIDED BY THE OWNER (language decision, not a bug-fix detail): component ORDER IS part of a named tuple's type identity. Every tuple has a CANONICAL POSITIONAL REPRESENTATION numbering components _1.._n by DEFINITION order, so (a: Int64, b: String) is (_1: Int64, _2: String) and (b: String, a: Int64) is (_1: String, _2: Int64) -- DIFFERENT TYPES. Permutation is refused at every position; names still participate, so (a: Int64, b: String) != (Int64, String) still holds (004 rule 4). WIDTH survives PREFIX-ONLY: dropping a TRAILING component preserves every retained component's canonical position, dropping a MIDDLE one renumbers what follows (c moving _3 -> _2), so (a,b,c) </: (a,c). NOTE this is STRICTER than the subsequence rule I first measured, which allowed the middle drop. DOC CONTRADICTION FOUND AND FIXED (the reason to check the spec BEFORE coding): kernel-language.md said permutation and width ARE subtyping rules for name-read data tuples and spelled out a permuted arrow as ACCEPTED, while CLAUDE.md said source order IS its identity, and proposal 004 was SILENT on order. I built and nearly shipped a change against CLAUDE.md's claim without checking the spec; the two tests it broke were SPEC'D BEHAVIOR, not collateral. All three documents now agree. COST, as measured: exactly 2 deliberate tests asserted the permutation is legal and both are now inverted -- wi782 permuted_tuple_typed_parameter_still_applies (-> _is_refused, plus a new in-order control) and wi488 named_tuple_reordered_component_abstracting_producer_flagged (split: an IN-ORDER named-tuple gate test, since every other fixture in that file is positional, plus a mismatch-before-the-gate test). A third, wi784 wrong_arity_application_is_still_refused_with_the_binder_count, MOVED FROM EVAL TO LOAD -- same invariant (the diagnostic names the binder-derived count, not a hardcoded 1), earlier stage.", at: "2026-07-21T12:55:06Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Two independent defects fixed, both measured end-to-end before and after: (1) ORDER IS IDENTITY -- align_named_tuple_fields' ByName arm went from an order-insensitive by-name lookup to a slot-by-slot zip with names required to agree at each slot, prefix width only; (2) THE Function SLOT NOW CHECKS ITS ARGUMENTS -- positional_arg_expectations is one total reader over BOTH callable spellings, returning Slots/CountMismatch/NoVerdict, replacing two inline branches with a hand-rolled arity error each. NoVerdict names the one genuine absence so it cannot read as 'handled'. /code-review ON MY OWN CHANGE FOUND 11 ISSUES, 3 of them mine and fixed here: (a) the count check compared A's components against the POSITIONAL count while the caller rendered the TOTAL, so a fully-labelled call f(a: 1, b: 2) produced 'expected ... or 2 ..., got 2 arguments' -- the expected-X-got-X shape WI-795 already fixed once -- AND preempted the accurate 'no parameter names to bind a label to' error; now returns NoVerdict when labels are present. (b) prefix width with an EMPTY expected list zipped to nothing and vacuously agreed, so ANY tuple conformed to the unit type () and then trapped against a nullary pattern -- pre-existing, but newly load-bearing on the prefix-width rule this ticket wrote into the spec; unit now relates to unit only. (c) the rewritten wi784 assertion was contains(\"2\") && contains(\"3\"), satisfied incidentally by a span like 3:2; now asserts the phrases. Both new guards CONTROL-TESTED by disabling them (2 tests fail) rather than assumed. /simplify also caught a comment I shipped claiming the two TupleAlign modes differ in EXACTLY ONE way -- there are TWO, and granting ByName the missing one (the synthetic _1.._n escape) would relate (a: Int64, b: String) to (Int64, String), reintroducing a variant of this bug. DOCS: kernel-language.md sections 4.5 / Arrow types / Function-equivalence / 6.7, proposal 004 rule 4a, and CLAUDE.md all reconciled -- they had CONTRADICTED each other, which is what nearly made me ship a spec-violating change. A late catch: my first pass at the docs said every tuple has a canonical positional representation numbering components _1.._n, which is NOT the implemented rule and cannot be -- read literally it erases names and collapses (a: A) into (_1: A), which 004 rule 4 refuses and positional_tuple_does_not_conform_to_a_named_one pins. Corrected in all five places to 'a component is identified by its name AND its position'. TESTS: 14 in wi788_tuple_component_order_identity_test, 8 of which fail against the parent (verified by control run); 3 inverted/moved elsewhere (wi782, wi488, wi784). Full workspace 3267 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh. FOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-798 (double extract_type per Function application), WI-799 (TupleAlign should name the discipline as a policy struct; also unblocks unify_named_tuple's asymmetric prefix width on an EQUALITY relation), WI-800 (thread_expected_tuple_field still order-blind, mis-aims diagnostics), WI-801 (typer pivots on A's component count while eval pivots on the callee's binder arity -- not enrolled in the Pattern::binder_arity one-owner discipline), WI-802 (\"anthill.prelude.Function\" hand-compared at 3 sites, silent failure mode).", at: "2026-07-21T13:39:37Z")
